1. Optimized Linux File System Layout for 50 GB Server
A separate partition should be allocated for logs to prevent disk exhaustion.
/boot – 1 GB
/ – 8 GB
/var/log – 10 GB
/home – 10 GB
/opt – 7 GB
Swap – 4 GB
Free space – 10 GB
Total = 50 GB
Use logrotate to rotate and compress logs. Monitor disk usage using `df` and `du`. The separate 10 GB `/var/log` partition ensures logs cannot fill the entire disk.


2. Kernel-Level Optimization for High CPU Workloads
The following techniques can reduce context switching and improve performance:
CPU Affinity:Assign processes to specific CPU cores.
Scheduler Tuning:Adjust process priorities.
Real-Time Scheduling:Use `SCHED_FIFO` and `SCHED_RR`.
CPU Isolation:Reserve CPU cores for real-time tasks.
Kernel Modules:Remove unnecessary modules.
Disable Services:Stop unnecessary background services.
Kernel Tuning:Optimize suitable kernel parameters.
These techniques improve CPU efficiency and support real-time processing without hardware upgrades.


